sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-17> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Local-improvement procedures get stuck when satisfying discrete constraints" ;
    dct:description "For discrete constraint systems the landscape of constraint violations is almost always jagged, so iterative procedures that only move downhill almost invariably get stuck far from a solution, random modifications help only slowly, and no speed-up techniques work well across broad classes of systems; hence natural systems are unlikely to come close to satisfying such constraints." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Problem of Satisfying Constraints” (from p. 342), p. 348. Scope: iterative and annealing-like procedures for discrete constraint satisfaction, and natural systems assumed to satisfy constraints" ;
    dct:type "computational-observation" .
}

